# The daily list work: mosdns-cdnctl update-lists --automatic.
#
# It ALWAYS reports whether the China domain list this router reads is still the one
# the reviewed source publishes -- that report is the only place this machine's
# answer about its own source is written down. Then it refreshes exactly what
# /etc/mosdns/policy.yaml says to refresh, which by default is nothing at all.
#
# Both switches are off in the shipped policy, and that is the decision rather than
# an omission: data/cn is curated by hand, so ANY upstream commit moves the split
# between domestic and foreign names, and accepting one unattended is a routing
# change nobody reviewed. An operator who has decided they want that trade sets
# lists.china.automatic in the policy; lists.cloudflare.automatic is a separate
# switch because refreshing which CDN edge an answer is rewritten to is a much
# smaller thing than changing which names take the foreign branch.
#
# What it can write is bounded by ReadWritePaths below, and it takes the control
# lock for as long as a publication takes -- which is why SuccessExitStatus=4 is
# here now. The lock is what stops this job from publishing a pair while the router
# is being reconfigured, and a daily job that finds the lock held is a daily job
# that did the right thing.
#
# It is Type=oneshot, it is not enabled, and its timer starts it. The TIMER is
# generated from the policy's schedule by `mosdns-cdnctl render`; this service is
# not generated, and the two are edited separately for that reason.

# VERSION FLOOR: systemd 249, the oldest supported release (Ubuntu 22.04). The
# newest directive in this file is ProtectProc= and ProcSubset=, both 247. A
# directive newer than 249 must not be added here without checking the floor, and
# `systemd-analyze verify` cannot check it for you: it runs against whatever
# systemd the build host has (259 on the machine this was written on), so it
# accepts a 255 directive that Ubuntu 22.04 would refuse at boot. Proving the
# floor is a container run -- the spec's isolation testing, not this file.

[Unit]
Description=Daily list work: report drift, and refresh what the policy says to refresh
Documentation=man:mosdns-cdnctl(1)
After=network.target

[Service]
Type=oneshot
User=mosdns-router-cdn
Group=mosdns-router
ExecStart=/usr/lib/mosdns-router/mosdns-cdnctl update-lists --automatic
# The budget is arithmetic, not a preference, and the arithmetic is this: EVERY
# request this command makes is capped by the command's own client timeout
# (updateListTimeout, 60 seconds, cmd/mosdns-cdnctl/update_lists.go), so a run can
# take at most (requests x 60s) and a unit that budgets less than that gets a
# SIGKILL instead of the report. The 90-second default for a oneshot would cut a
# slow origin off before it could report that it was slow, which is the whole
# reason this directive exists and is not a consequence of the number below.
#
# At most 20 requests, and that figure is MEASURED rather than counted by hand:
# cmd/mosdns-cdnctl/update_lists_budget_test.go drives `update-lists --automatic`
# through every path against a fake origin that records each request, and 20 is
# what the most expensive one costs. The shape that reaches it is the FULLY
# ENABLED cold start -- a machine with nothing published, BOTH policy switches on,
# and upstream committing while the run is in flight, which is what makes the
# second refresh in one run publish rather than report already-at.
#
# So 20 x 60 = 1200, and this is 1260: the arithmetic plus one more
# request-timeout for everything a run does that is NOT a request -- extracting a
# source tarball, converting and sorting a list of 110,551 rules, rendering the
# range prefix list, and replacing two files by two renames under the control
# lock. Generous, and free: this budget only ever binds when an origin is slow.
#
# The SHIPPED DEFAULT is far cheaper, and it is what nearly every machine runs,
# because both switches are off in configs/policy.yaml: the drift report alone
# costs 6 requests when both pinned sources have moved and 2 when neither has.
# One TimeoutStartSec has to cover the larger of the two, so it covers 20 -- and
# the comment says both, because "two budgets and one directive" is the honest
# description, and a comment quoting only the cheap one is how the 180 this
# replaced came to describe a command that no longer runs.
#
# A change that adds a request has to change this number with it. The gate is in
# cmd/mosdns-cdnctl, it re-reads THIS file, and it goes red when a path costs more
# than 20 requests, when the per-request timeout changes, or when this stops
# covering the product.
TimeoutStartSec=1260
# Exit 4 is the control lock being held. This unit takes that lock now, so the exit
# is reachable, and excusing it is the difference between "the daily job skipped
# because the router was being reconfigured" and "the daily job failed" -- which is
# the outcome this whole feature has to be able to produce honestly.
SuccessExitStatus=4
#
# Exit 3 is NOT excused. A run that produced no report at all is information rather
# than noise, including the documented transient of a check that caught a
# publication in flight: an operator sees one failed day and asks why, and that is
# the outcome the drift report exists to produce.
CapabilityBoundingSet=

# What it may write. Two directories, both the ones this package's own state
# directories, and nothing under /etc -- which is why the policy is read and never
# rewritten by this job: a scheduled job that could edit the configuration that
# tells it what to do is a job that can eventually tell itself to do anything.
ReadWritePaths=/var/lib/mosdns/lists /var/lib/mosdns/runtime
#
# A umask, because it creates files now. 0007, the same as the other two units that
# write, so the files it publishes are as private as the ones they publish.
UMask=0007

# The same sandbox as the other four. This command reads the policy and two documents
# under /etc, reads and writes the state directories above, makes HTTPS requests, and
# holds a lock for as long as a publication takes. It uses a temporary file beside
# each file it replaces, no device, no kernel setting, no process list and no
# namespace, and it changes no identity, no clock and no real-time priority.
NoNewPrivileges=true
PrivateTmp=true
PrivateDevices=true
ProtectSystem=strict
ProtectHome=true
ProtectKernelTunables=true
ProtectKernelModules=true
ProtectKernelLogs=true
ProtectControlGroups=true
ProtectClock=true
ProtectHostname=true
ProtectProc=invisible
ProcSubset=pid
LockPersonality=true
RestrictNamespaces=true
RestrictRealtime=true
RestrictSUIDSGID=true
SystemCallArchitectures=native

# It resolves the repository's host name and reads the CA store under /etc. It
# enumerates no interface and opens no UNIX socket of its own, but the system
# resolver may be one, so AF_UNIX is kept.
RestrictAddressFamilies=AF_UNIX AF_INET AF_INET6
